NAME
       grubby - list and change the kernels the boot loader starts

SYNOPSIS
       grubby --default-kernel | --default-index | --default-title
       grubby --info=KERNEL
       grubby --set-default=KERNEL | --set-default-index=INDEX
       grubby --update-kernel=KERNEL [--args="ARGS"] [--remove-args="ARGS"]

DESCRIPTION
       On RHEL 9 every kernel in the GRUB menu has its own small file in
       /boot/loader/entries (the Boot Loader Specification, "BLS", layout).
       Each file holds the kernel's title, version, the paths of the kernel
       and its initramfs, and an "options" line with the kernel command line.
       grubby is Red Hat's tool for reading and changing those files, so you
       never have to edit them by hand and you do not need to rebuild
       grub.cfg afterwards: GRUB reads the entries directly at every boot.

       Wherever an option takes a KERNEL you can give:

       ALL       every entry (only for --info and --update-kernel);

       DEFAULT   the entry that boots by default;

       a path    such as /boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-503.14.1.el9_5.x86_64;

       a number  the entry's index (0 is the first, normally the newest).

       Changes made with --update-kernel take effect at the next boot. Check
       the running kernel's command line with cat /proc/cmdline.

OPTIONS
       --default-kernel
              Print the path of the kernel that boots by default.

       --default-index
              Print the index of the default entry.

       --default-title
              Print the title of the default entry, as shown in the menu.

       --info=KERNEL
              Show the entry (or every entry, with ALL): index, kernel, args
              (the command line apart from root=), root, initrd, title and id.

       --set-default=KERNEL
              Make this kernel the one that boots by default. grubby writes
              the entry's id to saved_entry in /boot/grub2/grubenv.

       --set-default-index=INDEX
              The same, choosing the entry by its number.

       --update-kernel=KERNEL
              Change the command line of the chosen entries; use it with
              --args and/or --remove-args.

       --args="ARG ..."
              Arguments to add. An argument that is already there with another
              value (quiet, or crashkernel=...) is replaced, not doubled.

       --remove-args="ARG ..."
              Arguments to remove. A name alone (rhgb) removes it whatever its
              value.

NOTES
       --update-kernel=ALL also updates GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in
       /etc/default/grub, so kernels installed later get the same arguments.
       With DEFAULT or a single kernel only that entry changes.

       The other way to change the command line for every kernel is to edit
       GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in /etc/default/grub and run
       "grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg --update-bls-cmdline".

EXAMPLES
       Which kernel boots by default, and with what command line?
              # grubby --default-kernel
              /boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-503.14.1.el9_5.x86_64
              # grubby --info=DEFAULT

       Add an argument to every kernel, and remove two others:
              # grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args="audit=1"
              # grubby --update-kernel=ALL --remove-args="rhgb quiet"

       Boot an older kernel by default:
              # grubby --info=ALL | grep -E '^(index|kernel)'
              # grubby --set-default=/boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-427.13.1.el9_4.x86_64

       Check after a reboot:
              # cat /proc/cmdline

FILES
       /boot/loader/entries/*.conf
              One file per menu entry.

       /boot/grub2/grubenv
              Holds saved_entry, the default entry.

       /etc/default/grub
              The settings grub2-mkconfig uses.

SEE ALSO
       grub2-mkconfig(8), grub2-set-default(8), grub2-editenv(1), kernel-command-line(7)
